1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,119
It's Halloween night. Your doorbell rings for

2
00:00:03,019 --> 00:00:04,309
the twentieth time.

3
00:00:04,509 --> 00:00:05,802
You open the door,

4
00:00:05,702 --> 00:00:09,184
smile, and drop candy into a little pumpkin bucket.

5
00:00:09,384 --> 00:00:11,682
You're wearing a costume you made from

6
00:00:11,582 --> 00:00:12,607
your own closet.

7
00:00:12,807 --> 00:00:15,852
And this whole night cost you... forty-two dollars.

8
00:00:16,252 --> 00:00:18,887
Across town, your friend Mia is getting

9
00:00:18,787 --> 00:00:20,056
ready for a party.

10
00:00:20,256 --> 00:00:22,796
Her costume? One hundred eighty dollars.

11
00:00:22,696 --> 00:00:24,015
She'll wear it once.

12
00:00:24,215 --> 00:00:26,677
Plus a second outfit for Saturday.

13
00:00:26,577 --> 00:00:30,010
Plus new decorations. Plus a pumpkin patch trip.

14
00:00:30,210 --> 00:00:32,729
And she's excited. Really excited.

15
00:00:33,129 --> 00:00:34,917
When she adds it all up next

16
00:00:34,817 --> 00:00:36,544
week, the number will hurt.

17
00:00:36,744 --> 00:00:38,922
Four hundred and ten dollars.

18
00:00:38,822 --> 00:00:39,997
On one holiday.

19
00:00:40,197 --> 00:00:41,205
And most of it?

20
00:00:41,105 --> 00:00:43,746
It's sitting on her credit card right now.

21
00:00:43,946 --> 00:00:45,801
Let that sink in for a second.

22
00:00:46,201 --> 00:00:47,704
Here's the crazy part.

23
00:00:47,904 --> 00:00:50,249
At the party, nobody can tell which

24
00:00:50,149 --> 00:00:52,943
costume cost one hundred eighty dollars...

25
00:00:52,843 --> 00:00:54,611
and which one cost twelve.

26
00:00:54,811 --> 00:00:57,515
Everyone just has fun. Including you.

27
00:00:57,715 --> 00:01:00,040
Nobody checks the price tag on your hat.

28
00:01:00,440 --> 00:01:02,261
And Mia isn't unusual.

29
00:01:02,461 --> 00:01:06,036
The National Retail Federation expected Americans to

30
00:01:05,936 --> 00:01:08,308
spend a record thirteen and a half

31
00:01:08,208 --> 00:01:10,914
billion dollars on Halloween this year.

32
00:01:11,114 --> 00:01:14,160
That's about one hundred fifteen dollars per person.

33
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:15,931
A new record. Again.

34
00:01:16,331 --> 00:01:18,522
And here's how it usually happens.

35
00:01:18,722 --> 00:01:20,665
It's never one big purchase.

36
00:01:20,865 --> 00:01:22,894
A ten-dollar decoration here.

37
00:01:22,794 --> 00:01:25,089
A fifteen-dollar candy run there.

38
00:01:25,289 --> 00:01:27,848
Little 'it's just Halloween' moments...

39
00:01:27,748 --> 00:01:29,172
and they add up fast.

40
00:01:29,572 --> 00:01:30,816
So stay with me.

41
00:01:31,016 --> 00:01:33,425
I'll show you exactly how you enjoyed

42
00:01:33,325 --> 00:01:36,108
the same Halloween for forty-two dollars...

43
00:01:36,308 --> 00:01:38,601
and the one thing you'll do tomorrow

44
00:01:38,501 --> 00:01:41,159
morning that makes next year even cheaper.

45
00:01:41,359 --> 00:01:43,424
No guilt. No boring rules.

46
00:01:43,324 --> 00:01:44,859
Just smarter moves.

47
00:01:45,259 --> 00:01:48,259
Let's start with the biggest one. The costume.

48
00:01:48,459 --> 00:01:51,823
Costumes are where Americans spend the most on Halloween.

49
00:01:52,023 --> 00:01:54,997
About four point four billion dollars this year.

50
00:01:55,197 --> 00:01:58,194
And most of them get worn one single night.

51
00:01:58,594 --> 00:02:00,521
But before you bought anything,

52
00:02:00,421 --> 00:02:01,995
you did one simple thing.

53
00:02:02,195 --> 00:02:03,757
You opened your closet.

54
00:02:03,657 --> 00:02:06,109
And you asked: what can I already be?

55
00:02:06,309 --> 00:02:07,835
An old flannel shirt,

56
00:02:07,735 --> 00:02:09,328
jeans and a straw hat?

57
00:02:09,228 --> 00:02:10,617
That's a scarecrow.

58
00:02:10,817 --> 00:02:13,052
You didn't need a fancy costume.

59
00:02:12,952 --> 00:02:14,320
You needed an idea.

60
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:16,694
A white shirt and a little face

61
00:02:16,594 --> 00:02:18,750
paint? That's a ghost or a zombie.

62
00:02:18,950 --> 00:02:22,220
A black sweater and cat ears? Done. Seriously.

63
00:02:22,420 --> 00:02:24,848
The best costumes are often the simple

64
00:02:24,748 --> 00:02:27,054
ones people recognize in one second.

65
00:02:27,254 --> 00:02:28,889
No sewing. No stress.

66
00:02:29,289 --> 00:02:31,055
You spent twelve dollars.

67
00:02:30,955 --> 00:02:32,654
One prop. The straw hat.

68
00:02:32,854 --> 00:02:35,091
Mia spent one hundred eighty.

69
00:02:34,991 --> 00:02:36,859
Same party. Same photos.

70
00:02:37,059 --> 00:02:40,234
That's a one hundred sixty-eight dollar difference...

71
00:02:40,134 --> 00:02:41,279
from one decision.

72
00:02:41,479 --> 00:02:42,663
Think about that.

73
00:02:43,063 --> 00:02:45,096
Don't want to make one yourself?

74
00:02:44,996 --> 00:02:46,546
There are other options.

75
00:02:46,746 --> 00:02:48,672
A costume swap with friends.

76
00:02:48,572 --> 00:02:51,607
Everyone brings last year's costume and takes

77
00:02:51,507 --> 00:02:52,650
a different one.

78
00:02:52,850 --> 00:02:55,143
Free. And honestly... kind of fun.

79
00:02:55,343 --> 00:02:57,508
You walk in with one costume and

80
00:02:57,408 --> 00:02:59,056
walk out with a new one.

81
00:02:59,456 --> 00:03:01,620
Or check a thrift store first.

82
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:04,786
Lots of costumes there were worn exactly once.

83
00:03:04,986 --> 00:03:06,373
And if you have kids,

84
00:03:06,273 --> 00:03:08,029
this one matters even more.

85
00:03:08,229 --> 00:03:11,119
Kids outgrow costumes every single year.

86
00:03:11,019 --> 00:03:13,491
A swap with other parents can save

87
00:03:13,391 --> 00:03:14,537
you real money.

88
00:03:14,737 --> 00:03:18,285
Last year's dinosaur becomes this year's dinosaur...

89
00:03:18,185 --> 00:03:19,611
for a different kid.

90
00:03:20,011 --> 00:03:21,794
Move number two. Candy.

91
00:03:21,994 --> 00:03:25,094
Ninety-six percent of people planning to celebrate

92
00:03:24,994 --> 00:03:26,415
said they'd buy candy.

93
00:03:26,615 --> 00:03:28,311
And this is where a lot of

94
00:03:28,211 --> 00:03:29,845
money quietly disappears.

95
00:03:30,045 --> 00:03:32,331
Not because candy is expensive.

96
00:03:32,231 --> 00:03:34,024
Because nobody plans it.

97
00:03:34,424 --> 00:03:36,920
Mia bought candy three times this month.

98
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:38,719
Once in early October...

99
00:03:38,619 --> 00:03:39,969
and then she ate it.

100
00:03:40,169 --> 00:03:41,873
Then again two weeks later.

101
00:03:41,773 --> 00:03:43,417
And then she ate that too.

102
00:03:43,617 --> 00:03:45,370
Then one more time today,

103
00:03:45,270 --> 00:03:47,684
at full price, at the corner store.

104
00:03:48,084 --> 00:03:49,454
You did it differently.

105
00:03:49,654 --> 00:03:51,376
First, you made a guess.

106
00:03:51,276 --> 00:03:54,215
How many kids came last year? About sixty.

107
00:03:54,415 --> 00:03:58,197
Two pieces each. That's around one hundred twenty pieces.

108
00:03:58,397 --> 00:04:00,515
Simple math. Two minutes. Done.

109
00:04:00,915 --> 00:04:03,046
Then you looked at the little price

110
00:04:02,946 --> 00:04:04,033
tag on the shelf.

111
00:04:03,933 --> 00:04:05,658
The one with the unit price.

112
00:04:05,858 --> 00:04:08,447
Big bags are usually cheaper per piece

113
00:04:08,347 --> 00:04:09,496
than small ones.

114
00:04:09,696 --> 00:04:11,582
But you only buy the big bag

115
00:04:11,482 --> 00:04:13,496
if you'll actually use it all.

116
00:04:13,696 --> 00:04:16,265
Store brands and mixed packs are often

117
00:04:16,165 --> 00:04:18,799
cheaper too. Kids don't check the logo.

118
00:04:19,199 --> 00:04:21,589
And here's the rule that saved you the most.

119
00:04:21,789 --> 00:04:24,657
You bought the candy on Halloween week.

120
00:04:24,557 --> 00:04:26,148
Not in early October.

121
00:04:26,348 --> 00:04:28,504
Candy you don't have in the house...

122
00:04:28,404 --> 00:04:29,818
is candy you don't eat.

123
00:04:30,018 --> 00:04:32,125
Wait until the week of Halloween.

124
00:04:32,025 --> 00:04:33,767
Your wallet will thank you.

125
00:04:34,167 --> 00:04:37,577
Your candy budget? Twenty-four dollars. One trip.

126
00:04:37,777 --> 00:04:40,067
Mia's? Forty-five. Three trips.

127
00:04:40,267 --> 00:04:42,118
And she's still buying more tonight.

128
00:04:42,318 --> 00:04:44,323
Same kids. Same smiles.

129
00:04:44,723 --> 00:04:46,957
Move number three. Decorations.

130
00:04:47,157 --> 00:04:50,007
Americans planned to spend about four point

131
00:04:49,907 --> 00:04:53,653
three billion dollars on Halloween decorations this year.

132
00:04:53,853 --> 00:04:55,348
But here's the thing.

133
00:04:55,248 --> 00:04:57,009
Decorations don't expire.

134
00:04:57,209 --> 00:04:58,522
You buy them once.

135
00:04:58,422 --> 00:05:00,342
You can use them for years.

136
00:05:00,742 --> 00:05:02,586
Last year, after Halloween,

137
00:05:02,486 --> 00:05:04,847
you packed everything into one bin.

138
00:05:05,047 --> 00:05:06,933
You drew a little pumpkin on the

139
00:05:06,833 --> 00:05:08,831
lid. And you put it in the closet.

140
00:05:09,031 --> 00:05:11,045
So this year, you didn't buy the

141
00:05:10,945 --> 00:05:13,198
same skeleton... for the third time.

142
00:05:13,598 --> 00:05:16,413
Mia? She couldn't find last year's decorations.

143
00:05:16,613 --> 00:05:20,027
They're somewhere. Behind the vacuum cleaner probably.

144
00:05:20,227 --> 00:05:23,261
So she bought them all again. Ninety dollars.

145
00:05:23,461 --> 00:05:25,368
Sound familiar? Be honest.

146
00:05:25,768 --> 00:05:27,869
You added a few cheap touches too.

147
00:05:28,069 --> 00:05:30,557
Paper bats cut from black paper.

148
00:05:30,457 --> 00:05:33,318
A few glass jars with candles inside.

149
00:05:33,218 --> 00:05:34,886
Leaves from the yard.

150
00:05:35,086 --> 00:05:37,721
It looked cozy. It looked like Halloween.

151
00:05:37,621 --> 00:05:39,390
And it cost almost nothing.

152
00:05:39,790 --> 00:05:41,715
Move number four. The pumpkin.

153
00:05:41,915 --> 00:05:43,867
Pumpkin patches are fun.

154
00:05:43,767 --> 00:05:46,181
Hayrides, photos, apple cider.

155
00:05:46,381 --> 00:05:48,320
But if you just need a pumpkin

156
00:05:48,220 --> 00:05:50,588
for the porch... the grocery store is

157
00:05:50,488 --> 00:05:51,753
often much cheaper.

158
00:05:51,953 --> 00:05:53,296
Nothing wrong with that,

159
00:05:53,196 --> 00:05:54,488
if it's in your budget.

160
00:05:54,888 --> 00:05:56,376
You paid six dollars.

161
00:05:56,276 --> 00:05:58,822
You carved it with friends on Sunday.

162
00:05:59,022 --> 00:06:00,926
And you roasted the seeds in the

163
00:06:00,826 --> 00:06:02,279
oven with a little salt.

164
00:06:02,479 --> 00:06:04,779
A free snack. Seriously, try it.

165
00:06:05,179 --> 00:06:07,505
Mia went to a pumpkin patch with

166
00:06:07,405 --> 00:06:09,035
an entry fee, parking,

167
00:06:08,935 --> 00:06:11,400
and a pumpkin priced by the pound.

168
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:12,786
Thirty dollars.

169
00:06:12,986 --> 00:06:14,671
It was a nice afternoon.

170
00:06:14,571 --> 00:06:17,245
But it wasn't the only way to have one.

171
00:06:17,645 --> 00:06:19,572
Move number five. The party.

172
00:06:19,772 --> 00:06:22,117
You didn't host everything yourself.

173
00:06:22,017 --> 00:06:23,239
You did a potluck.

174
00:06:23,439 --> 00:06:24,735
You made the chili.

175
00:06:24,635 --> 00:06:26,748
Everyone else brought one thing.

176
00:06:27,148 --> 00:06:28,965
Ten people. Ten dishes.

177
00:06:28,865 --> 00:06:30,160
One great table.

178
00:06:30,360 --> 00:06:31,762
And the cost for you?

179
00:06:31,662 --> 00:06:34,057
The ingredients for one pot of chili.

180
00:06:34,257 --> 00:06:37,526
Nobody felt cheap. Everyone felt included.

181
00:06:37,726 --> 00:06:40,098
And honestly? It was the best party

182
00:06:39,998 --> 00:06:40,877
of the year.

183
00:06:41,277 --> 00:06:43,647
Then you set one simple rule for the night.

184
00:06:43,847 --> 00:06:45,735
You leave the credit card at home.

185
00:06:45,935 --> 00:06:47,330
You bring a little cash.

186
00:06:47,230 --> 00:06:49,056
And when it's gone... it's gone.

187
00:06:49,256 --> 00:06:52,602
No late-night pizza order you'll regret tomorrow.

188
00:06:53,002 --> 00:06:54,373
Now let's add it up.

189
00:06:54,573 --> 00:06:56,883
Costume: twelve dollars. Candy:

190
00:06:56,783 --> 00:06:58,737
twenty-four. Pumpkin: six.

191
00:06:58,937 --> 00:07:01,706
Decorations: zero. You already had them.

192
00:07:02,106 --> 00:07:04,450
Your Halloween: forty-two dollars.

193
00:07:04,650 --> 00:07:07,337
Mia's Halloween: four hundred and ten.

194
00:07:07,537 --> 00:07:09,113
Same night. Same fun.

195
00:07:09,013 --> 00:07:12,277
Three hundred sixty-eight dollars difference.

196
00:07:12,677 --> 00:07:14,884
And if Mia only pays the minimum

197
00:07:14,784 --> 00:07:15,871
on that card...

198
00:07:16,071 --> 00:07:19,810
that Halloween follows her around for months. With interest.

199
00:07:20,010 --> 00:07:21,562
Halloween is one night.

200
00:07:21,462 --> 00:07:23,899
The bill shouldn't last until spring.

201
00:07:24,099 --> 00:07:25,480
Every month she waits,

202
00:07:25,380 --> 00:07:27,052
the interest keeps growing.

203
00:07:27,452 --> 00:07:29,586
Okay. Now the move I promised you.

204
00:07:29,786 --> 00:07:32,223
Tomorrow morning is November first.

205
00:07:32,423 --> 00:07:34,806
And that's when Halloween stores start to

206
00:07:34,706 --> 00:07:35,976
clear everything out.

207
00:07:36,376 --> 00:07:40,937
Decorations, costumes, lights... often half off on November first.

208
00:07:41,137 --> 00:07:43,957
And sometimes even cheaper a few days later.

209
00:07:44,157 --> 00:07:45,700
So you go with a list.

210
00:07:45,600 --> 00:07:47,798
Not a cart full of random stuff.

211
00:07:47,998 --> 00:07:49,825
Not because they're generous.

212
00:07:49,725 --> 00:07:52,683
Because they need the shelf space for Christmas.

213
00:07:53,083 --> 00:07:55,614
You buy the one thing you'll definitely

214
00:07:55,514 --> 00:07:56,487
use next year.

215
00:07:56,687 --> 00:07:58,991
Maybe a string of orange lights.

216
00:07:58,891 --> 00:08:01,607
Maybe a kid's costume one size bigger.

217
00:08:01,807 --> 00:08:03,575
And it goes straight into the bin

218
00:08:03,475 --> 00:08:04,990
with the pumpkin on the lid.

219
00:08:05,390 --> 00:08:06,871
And the leftover candy?

220
00:08:07,071 --> 00:08:09,762
After Halloween, a lot of stores mark

221
00:08:09,662 --> 00:08:10,813
candy down too.

222
00:08:11,013 --> 00:08:13,397
If you're going to buy chocolate anyway

223
00:08:13,297 --> 00:08:15,623
in November... that's when you buy it.

224
00:08:15,823 --> 00:08:17,510
Just don't buy it before.

225
00:08:17,910 --> 00:08:19,240
But here's the warning.

226
00:08:19,440 --> 00:08:21,825
A discount only saves money if you

227
00:08:21,725 --> 00:08:23,706
were going to buy it anyway.

228
00:08:23,906 --> 00:08:26,766
Fifty percent off something you don't need...

229
00:08:26,666 --> 00:08:28,851
is still a hundred percent wasted.

230
00:08:29,251 --> 00:08:30,929
And then you do one last thing.

231
00:08:31,129 --> 00:08:33,240
You open your banking app and create

232
00:08:33,140 --> 00:08:35,643
a little savings bucket called 'Halloween'.

233
00:08:35,843 --> 00:08:38,189
Ten dollars a month. Automatically.

234
00:08:38,389 --> 00:08:40,474
You named it, so you know exactly

235
00:08:40,374 --> 00:08:41,317
what it's for.

236
00:08:41,717 --> 00:08:44,308
By next October, there's one hundred twenty

237
00:08:44,208 --> 00:08:45,583
dollars sitting there.

238
00:08:45,783 --> 00:08:47,239
Cash. Already saved.

239
00:08:47,439 --> 00:08:50,794
So next Halloween costs you nothing extra.

240
00:08:50,694 --> 00:08:52,267
No card. No stress.

241
00:08:52,667 --> 00:08:54,348
It's called a sinking fund.

242
00:08:54,548 --> 00:08:56,437
You save a little every month for

243
00:08:56,337 --> 00:08:58,009
something you know is coming.

244
00:08:58,209 --> 00:09:01,624
Halloween. Christmas. Birthdays. Car insurance.

245
00:09:01,524 --> 00:09:03,529
None of them are surprises.

246
00:09:03,729 --> 00:09:05,320
You already know the dates.

247
00:09:05,220 --> 00:09:06,645
They're on the calendar.

248
00:09:07,045 --> 00:09:09,204
And they only feel like surprises...

249
00:09:09,104 --> 00:09:10,864
when you don't plan for them.

250
00:09:11,064 --> 00:09:13,725
That's why Mia feels broke every November.

251
00:09:13,925 --> 00:09:14,904
And you don't.

252
00:09:15,304 --> 00:09:16,776
So tonight, enjoy it.

253
00:09:16,976 --> 00:09:18,330
Hand out the candy.

254
00:09:18,230 --> 00:09:19,453
Eat a few pieces.

255
00:09:19,353 --> 00:09:20,905
Laugh at the costumes.

256
00:09:21,105 --> 00:09:22,436
You earned this night.

257
00:09:22,636 --> 00:09:24,740
Tomorrow is for the smart moves.

258
00:09:25,140 --> 00:09:27,269
And tomorrow, three small moves.

259
00:09:27,469 --> 00:09:30,261
Pack the decorations in one labeled bin.

260
00:09:30,461 --> 00:09:32,574
Buy only what you'll use next year,

261
00:09:32,474 --> 00:09:33,264
from a list.

262
00:09:33,464 --> 00:09:35,979
And start that ten-dollar Halloween fund.

263
00:09:36,379 --> 00:09:38,883
If this helped, subscribe for one simple

264
00:09:38,783 --> 00:09:40,145
money move every day.

265
00:09:40,345 --> 00:09:42,127
And tell me in the comments...

266
00:09:42,027 --> 00:09:44,088
what did you dress up as this year?

267
00:09:44,288 --> 00:09:47,364
Bonus points if it cost less than twelve dollars.
